    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0f58ad4d-7f2e-5f82-a45b-d443ba532270",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/f8/f88a8daa307b97157b5088ef25c469d7534cfc89f707dcd25eb880a406df4ae9.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$ is a simple root.\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- **III.** $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-1$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
